body:
This issue does not occur in animations where the timeline length is set to 1.0 and keys are placed at 1.0. This problem is caused by the time stored by the AnimationTree for each instance being registered in the dictionary as a Variant type, resulting in less precision than the original double. This appears to be causing a time difference between the previous frame and the current frame's animation in spite of these actual time are the same, resulting in progression occurring.

It can be resolved by lowering the precision of the time comparison function like:

**Animation.h**
```cpp
	static bool is_less_or_equal_approx(double a, double b) {
		return a < b || Math::is_equal_approx(a, b, 0.001);
	}

	static bool is_less_approx(double a, double b) {
		return a < b && !Math::is_equal_approx(a, b, 0.001);
	}

	static bool is_greater_or_equal_approx(double a, double b) {
		return a > b || Math::is_equal_approx(a, b, 0.001);
	}

	static bool is_greater_approx(double a, double b) {
		return a > b && !Math::is_equal_approx(a, b, 0.001);
	}
```

but I'm unsure if that is advisable. Ideally, increasing the precision of the time stored in the dictionary would be the correct fix.
